kids-learning-designer audit — surah + story pedagogy
Scope: UI/ (11 surah pages incl. ayat-al-kursi, 5 story pages, quran.html hub)
Report-only run, no edits made (worktree instruction).

=== 1. QUIZ QUALITY ===
Scripted audit (/tmp/quiz_audit.py) parsed every quiz-card block in all 16 pages
(11 surah pages x4 + 5 story pages x3 = 59 questions, full coverage confirmed).

LONGEST-OPTION GIVEAWAY RATE: 40/59 = 68%.
This is not noise — it is the dominant pattern across the whole site. A child (or a
tired parent skimming) can beat the quiz over two-thirds of the time by picking the
longest button without reading any of them.

Worst offenders (correct answer is >1.5x the length of every distractor):
- UI/surah-al-maun.html:285 — Q3: correct "Those who are careless and pray just to be
  seen" (47 chars) vs distractors 18/25/28 chars.
- UI/surah-al-ikhlas.html:253 — Q4: correct "Everyone needs Allah, but Allah needs no
  one" (44) vs 21/22/22.
- UI/surah-quraysh.html:252 — Q4: correct "Food from hunger, and safety from fear" (38)
  vs 15/15/22.
- UI/surah-al-kafirun.html:246 — Q1: correct "To take turns worshipping each other's
  gods" (43) vs 24/18/21.
- UI/surah-al-maun.html:297 — Q4: correct "Doing good just so people will see you" (38)
  vs 20/23/25.
- UI/ayat-al-kursi.html:267 (Q1 block start) — Q3/Q4 correct answers are the fullest,
  most explanatory options every time.

Recall-vs-understanding: most questions are straight recall of a fact stated verbatim
one paragraph above ("How many verses...", "What does the name mean...", "Which surah
is this..."). Very few require synthesis (e.g. surah-al-kafirun Q3 "You follow your
way, and I follow mine" tests grasp of the surah's message, not a lookup — that's the
model to replicate). Distractors are rarely silly/implausible (good — they're mostly
plausible near-misses), but because the correct answer is also the most descriptive
sentence, a child never has to distinguish them on content.

Answerable-without-reading: verse-count questions ("How many verses in Surah X?") and
"which surah is this" questions are answerable from the hero/meta chip at the top of
the page without engaging with the verses at all (e.g. surah-fatiha.html Q1, surah-al-
falaq.html Q2, surah-al-ikhlas.html Q1). These test page-scanning, not comprehension.

Fix: shuffle option order at render time (trivial JS, ~30 min) — this alone kills the
positional/length tell without rewriting any content. Separately, rewrite the ~15
worst offenders so correct and incorrect options are matched in length/register
(~2-3 hrs, content-scholar should co-own wording).

=== 2. MEMORIZATION PATH ===
Confirmed in UI/surah-fatiha.html:108-193 (verse-card pattern, identical across all
11 surah pages): every verse-card unconditionally renders Arabic (.arabic), English
transliteration (.transliteration), translation (.translation), AND an explanation
block, all visible simultaneously, with no toggle. Checked *.js/*.css for a
show/hide/toggle affordance on transliteration — none exists.

Effect on a memorizing child: transliteration is the path of least resistance to
"performing" the verse without ever decoding the Arabic script. A child who can lean
on Latin-letter transliteration will default to reading that, not the Arabic — by
week 2 they can "recite" fluently while still unable to read مَالِكِ يَوْمِ الدِّينِ
unaided. This directly matches the charter's stated known failure mode.

Concrete staged alternative (3 stages, toggle-driven, no new content needed —
just visibility state + a control):
  Stage 1 (Night 1, "Learn"): Arabic + transliteration + translation + explanation
  all shown, as today.
  Stage 2 (Night 2+, "Practice"): transliteration collapsed behind a tap
  ("🔤 Show pronunciation help") — Arabic + translation visible by default, child
  reads Arabic first and only reveals transliteration if stuck.
  Stage 3 (Night 5+/"Recite"): Arabic only, translation collapsed behind a tap;
  transliteration removed entirely from this mode. Explanation stays available on
  tap as a hint, not inline.
  Persist stage choice in localStorage per surah so the father doesn't have to
  reconfigure each night; default to Stage 1 for a surah never opened before.
Effort: medium (~1 day) — mostly CSS class toggling + a 3-state control + localStorage
per surah-slug; the HTML/data already carries every field needed, this is a render/
visibility change, not new content.

=== 3. COGNITIVE LOAD & LENGTH ===
Scripted word counts (/tmp/wordcount2.py), isolating intro+verses+full-surah-recap
("core lesson content" a father reads aloud) separately from fun-facts+tips:

  Surah                core   +tips  combined
  surah-al-ikhlas       493    145      638   <- shortest
  surah-al-asr          513    150      663
  surah-al-kawthar      523    152      675
  surah-quraysh         550    142      692
  surah-an-nasr         559    151      710
  surah-al-falaq        629    158      787
  surah-an-nas          633    146      779
  surah-al-kafirun      642    150      792
  surah-fatiha          688    123      811
  surah-al-maun         706    143      849
  ayat-al-kursi         767    153      920   <- longest, 44% more core text than
                                                 shortest, 30-40% above the group median

All 11 pages are in a fairly tight, plausible bedtime band (~640-920 words combined ≈
5-7 min read-aloud at a slow, expressive pace for a young child) — none are wildly
oversized. Ayat al-Kursi is the clear outlier on raw length, consistent with it also
being the longest single verse on the site (one continuous 50-word ayah vs. 3-7 short
verses elsewhere), and it's also structurally the densest (single unbroken verse,
no natural multi-verse pacing built in).

Natural break point for Ayat al-Kursi: the verse itself splits into recognizable
clauses (Allah's oneness/eternal-living → His knowledge → His Throne/dominion →
His greatness) — the site should chunk it into 3-4 shown-one-at-a-time phrase cards
(it may already partially do this in the verses-section; confirm and, if not,
build it) rather than presenting the full 50-word ayah as one block, and treat
"today we learned the first half" as a legitimate stopping point for night 1.
Effort: small for a content note now (flag this for content-scholar), medium
(~half day) if the phrase-by-phrase card breakdown needs building.

=== 4. PROGRESS & MOTIVATION ===
UI/quran.html:55-90+ — 11 `.story-select-card` entries, structurally identical
(thumbnail, title, meta chips, single "Start Learning →" button). No stars, no
percent-complete, no visited/completed state, no locking, verified by grep for
progress/star/unlock/completed/locked — zero hits in quran.html.

Contrast: UI/story-prophet-adam.html:54-78 has a persistent `.stars-counter` (id=
starsCounter/starCount) and a `.progress-bar`/`.progress-text` ("0% Complete"), plus
per-scene `.scene-reward` chips (+1/+2/+3 stars) and a `.video-unlock` reward gate at
the end (line 444). The story system already has the full motivational vocabulary
(stars, %, unlock) — surah pages have none of it, and it's the same child navigating
both experiences, so the inconsistency itself is confusing ("stories give me stars,
surahs don't — are surahs less important?").

Recommendation: port the story-page star/progress pattern onto surah pages
(1 star per quiz question correct + completion badge on quran.html cards showing
0-4 stars / "Recited to Dad ✓" checkbox a parent can tap), and change quran.html
cards from identical to state-aware: locked/dim until the previous surah in the
ramp is at least visited, checkmark or star-count badge once attempted, subtle
"Continue" highlight on the next unstarted one. This also directly serves difficulty-
ramp guidance (item 5) by making the recommended order visible instead of implicit.
Effort: medium-large (~1-2 days) — needs a shared localStorage schema (surah slug ->
stars/completed) reused by both quran.html cards and each surah page's quiz.

=== 5. DIFFICULTY RAMP ===
Stated order: Fatiha -> Ayat al-Kursi -> Al-Ikhlas -> Al-Falaq -> An-Nas -> Al-Kawthar
-> Al-Asr -> An-Nasr -> Al-Kafirun -> Al-Maun -> Quraysh.

Ayat al-Kursi at position 2 is wrong for a child, on two independent measures this
audit can point to:
  - It is the longest page on the site by word count (920 combined vs. 638-811 for
    everything else, see item 3) — a beginner's second-ever surah page souring them
    on length before they've built any reading stamina.
  - It is a single dense 50-word ayah with no verse-by-verse rhythm (contrast Fatiha's
    seven short, end-stopped verses) — much harder to chunk into a bedtime session.
  Traditionally Ayat al-Kursi is memorized precisely because of its weight and
  virtue, but that's an argument for teaching it once a child already has 3-4 short,
  successful surahs behind them (confidence + stamina), not as the first thing after
  Fatiha.

Recommended reordering: Fatiha -> Al-Ikhlas -> Al-Falaq -> An-Nas -> Al-Kawthar ->
Al-Asr -> Quraysh -> An-Nasr -> Al-Kafirun -> Al-Maun -> Ayat al-Kursi (moved to
last, as a "graduation" piece once every other card already has stars). This is a
sequencing/labeling change only (card order on quran.html + any "next surah" links),
no content rewrite. Effort: small (~1-2 hrs).

=== 6. PARENT AFFORDANCES ===
Grepped for "ask your child", "parent tip", "read aloud", "recap" across all HTML —
zero hits anywhere on the site. Checked UI/surah-fatiha.html:352-376
(.tips-section, "Tips for Memorization") as the closest existing feature: every tip
is phrased in second person addressed to the child ("You already recite it...",
"Ask Allah to help you...") — there is nothing addressed to the reading parent.

Missing, in priority order:
  1. A one-line "tonight's recap" the father can say before closing the page, e.g.
     under fun-facts or tips: "Tonight you and [child] learned: Al-Fatiha is 7
     verses and means 'The Opening.'" — gives the ritual a natural close and gives
     the parent something to repeat at breakfast.
  2. "Ask your child" prompts interleaved between verse-cards — a father reading
     aloud has no cue for when to pause and check in versus just reading straight
     through 5-7 verse-cards non-stop. E.g. after verse 3 of Fatiha: "💬 Ask: what
     does 'Ar-Rahman' mean?" This is cheap (one line per verse-card, content-scholar
     supplies wording) and directly supports active recall mid-session rather than
     only at the end-quiz.
  3. An explicit resume/stopping point — nothing marks "you can stop here for
     tonight" for pages long enough to need it (chiefly Ayat al-Kursi, item 3).
     Combine with the progress state (item 4): if a father closes the tab after
     verse 3 of 7, the star/progress system (once built) should reopen there next
     time rather than restarting the page.
  4. A "read this part yourself, Dad reads the rest" split isn't necessary yet, but
     becomes relevant once the memorization staging (item 2) exists.
Effort: (1) and (2) are small content+markup additions (~2-3 hrs combined, mostly
content-scholar's wording); (3) depends on the progress-state work in item 4.

